<https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1896-98/page/93> a crm:E22_Man-Made_Object,
        mbdiaries-ontology:Page ;
    rdfs:label "93"^^xsd:string ;
    crm:P129i_is_subject_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/diary/1896-98/document/93> ;
    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420766179/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """visit. I don’t know when I have ever enjoyed a winter so much, and I make Alys and Bertie green with the account of our intellectual feasts and fasts. Bertie longs to join in the hounding of the German Emperor and hopes there will still be some of him left in the Autumn. He has about three words too that he wants to say about Democracy …
I read  En route  in the train, and was much interested in all he said on his point of view and information – there was no eddy or agitation in the 13th or aesthetic water-tight compartment. Read  En Route  and then  La Tentation ,  et vous verrez, Messieurs et Dames! 
Give my affectionate regards to the Bel Ermite, and tell him that, 
94-95: two blank pages """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 93 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1896-98> .

